WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.450
- Hi everyone, welcome to our
Daily Homeroom live stream.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:29.150
This is a way that we're
trying to keep everyone

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.550
in touch during school closures.

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:34.390
It's a place for us to answer
any questions you have,

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.760
talk about how we can just
navigate this crisis together.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.300
We have a really exciting
guest today, Ray Dalio.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.300
I'll give him a more formal
introduction in a little bit.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.400
But before we get into the meat

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.780
of that conversation with Ray,

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:46.610
I'll just remind everyone Khan Academy

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.830
is a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:51.070
We exist because of
philanthropic donations

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:53.080
from folks like yourself.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:55.810
And I wanna give a special
shoutout to several corporations

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:59.220
who stepped up in the last
few weeks to make sure,

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.650
Khan Academy was running at a deficit even

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.080
before this crisis and
our deficit has grown

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.050
through the crisis,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.700
and special shoutout to Bank of America,

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.150
to Novartis, to Fastly,

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.030
to Google.org and AT&amp;T

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.120
for helping us respond to this crisis.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.390
But we still need more support,

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.180
so if you're in a position to do so,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.770
please think about making
a donation to Khan Academy.

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:23.450
But with that, I'd like
to introduce our guest

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.690
that I'm super excited about.

00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:29.830
Ray Dalio has been
something a hero of mine

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:31.390
back from my previous career

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.510
when I used to be a hedge fund analyst.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:35.060
For those of you all who don't know Ray,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:36.970
Ray is the founder of
Bridgewater Associates,

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.260
which is the largest
hedge fund in the world.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.800
And so I think we're gonna
have a really interesting

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.430
conversation about finance,
the economy that we're in,

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:47.410
and a lot of the work that
Ray and his wife, Barbara,

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.370
are doing around philanthropy
to try to help address issues

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.210
around education and other things.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:53.700
And so if anyone has questions,

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.890
feel free to come on Facebook and YouTube

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.300
on the message boards,
we have team members

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:58.280
looking at questions.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.570
Ask questions about the economy,

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:00.740
ask questions about hedge funds,

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.243
ask questions about education.

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.820
Ray's going to, and myself,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:07.970
will hopefully be able
to address a lot of them.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.693
So, Ray, thank you so much for joining.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.400
- It's always great to be with you.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.700
- And, Ray, maybe a good place to start,

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.010
and full disclosure, Ray
and Barbara have been

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.100
long-time supporters of Khan Academy

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.770
and philanthropy in a lot
of different dimensions,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:24.190
so thank you for that.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.880
But when you say "the largest
hedge fund in the world",

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.860
I think a lot of people
have heard of the word

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:30.477
hedge fund, but then they say,

00:02:30.477 --> 00:02:32.017
"What is a hedge fund?"

00:02:32.017 --> 00:02:33.630
(laughs) So what do you do?

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.180
How is that different than other types

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:36.930
of investment vehicles?

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.370
- Well, most investment vehicles

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:42.430
go up or down with the markets,

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.480
like the stock market goes up,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.540
they make money, stock market goes down,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:47.920
they lose money.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.570
Some go into bonds and so on.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.680
A hedge fund doesn't have a bias,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.270
so you can make money if it goes down,

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.180
you can make money if it goes up.

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.210
It just depends on whether
you can make money.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.850
So we can invest in all
the markets in the world,

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:07.040
and we can do it in a way
where if they went down

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.180
or went up, you have
opportunities to make money.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.520
The only thing that limits us

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:13.783
is our own abilities.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.660
- Yep.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:18.410
With the explanation I
always used to give to folks

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:19.660
when I was an analyst at a hedge fund

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.910
is hedge funds have more flexibility

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.320
to make more different
types of investments.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.030
Now, there's a huge variety of them,

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.050
of different strategies.

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.280
But to your point, they don't necessarily

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.510
just track the market type of thing.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:34.403
- Yes, and ours,

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.530
it's an extension of my
own personal passion,

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:43.293
which is to understand
how the world works,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.160
economically, every country.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.840
So we're in just about every country,

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.770
just about every market,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:53.820
and so we have to see the world

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.670
and try to piece it all together.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:57.203
So that's my passion.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.590
- And maybe that's a good place to start,

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:00.500
at a very high level,

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.620
because even before the
COVID crisis started,

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:04.000
this was many months ago,

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.210
you have been a very
vocal speaker and writer

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.100
about wealth inequality.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.820
And when you speak, people are listening

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.790
because, obviously, you
understand the economy,

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.210
you manage a lot of assets.

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:19.943
What's your view on this
issue of wealth inequality?

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.863
- Well, I think it comes
down to values, you know?

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.770
I was born in 1949.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.240
In 1945, we began a new world order.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.180
What I mean is we created
a new monetary system,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.040
the United States won the war,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.270
and we began an American era.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.723
And at that time,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.930
it was clear what an American dream was.

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:49.390
And so I grew up in a household

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.850
where I've got two parents,

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:54.033
my dad was a jazz musician,
lower-middle-class family.

00:04:55.414 --> 00:04:58.580
But the notion of equal opportunity

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.280
through good education, I
went to a public school,

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.770
and that notion of what was fair,

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:09.910
equal opportunity on a broad-base basis,

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.873
was what the American dream was.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.590
I'm not sure that everybody would know

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.390
what the American dream
is or state it that way,

00:05:19.390 --> 00:05:20.473
but in any case,

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:26.310
it's become lost to where
it certainly doesn't exist,

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.910
in that when we take
education, for example,

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.130
I looked the conditions

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:36.520
of the different top quintile, top 20%,

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.550
next 20%, and I found

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.450
that the top 40% of the population

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.420
spends on average five times as much money

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:49.690
on their kids' education
as the bottom 60%.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.960
And so through intimate
contact, and so on,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.930
we can see that there is
not just a wealth gap,

00:05:56.930 --> 00:05:58.850
there's an opportunity gap

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:02.070
and a productivity gap that's a problem.

00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:05.040
And it's a problem in
terms of its outcomes,

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.253
the wealth difference.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.630
But it's also a structural problem

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.630
in that all the people can't be,

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:15.260
have an equal shot at being productive.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.453
And when you see some of the conditions,

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:23.340
kids who are living in neighborhoods

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:27.340
of poverty and go to school to get food

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:28.980
because they don't have enough at home,

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:32.830
and the services, something's wrong.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:35.270
So I wrote a piece that's on LinkedIn,

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:40.270
which is Why and How Capitalism
Needs to be Reformed,

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.510
because I believe that,

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.230
first of all, if you
don't have a situation

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:47.180
where people have opportunity,

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.580
you're not only tapping, failing to tap

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.620
all the potential that exists,

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.140
which is uneconomic,

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.250
but you're also threatening the existence

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:59.030
of the system, and I think
that's coming to home

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.927
very clearly with this
downturn in the economy

00:07:01.927 --> 00:07:02.963
and the virus.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.130
- And to be clear, I'm
looking at some of your blog,

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.560
you wrote Why and How
Capitalism Needs to be Reformed,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.710
the data is very clear,
that's not under debate,

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.070
that inequality has gotten worse

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.200
over the last several decades.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.960
You have charts like, you
look at per capita GDP,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:18.860
and for the bottom 60% of earners,

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.780
it's grown much slower
than per capita GDP,

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.360
and for the top 40%,
it's grown much faster.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:25.340
And that leads to what
you just talked about,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.650
people at the upper quartiles

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:31.070
are able to spend more on
their children's education,

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.923
so that kind of becomes a
self-perpetuating thing.

00:07:35.380 --> 00:07:36.960
Why do you think this has been the case?

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:39.090
What are the structural
elements that have caused this?

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:41.863
And in your opinion, what needs to change?

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.150
- Yeah, great question,

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.940
because I don't think
it's anybody's evilness,

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:49.600
at least not many people's evilness,

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:50.850
it is structural.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:54.200
So, for example, the profit-making system

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:59.400
is a system in which it's
a very effective resource

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:03.340
allocation because if
what you are putting out

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.040
is worth more than what
it costs you to make it,

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:13.887
you grow and you get capital
that's invested in it,

00:08:13.887 --> 00:08:15.890
and it grows that way.

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:18.983
But it's not effective
in a number of ways.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.300
The profit-making system, for example,

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:27.823
is not effective in creating
educational resources well.

00:08:29.122 --> 00:08:34.020
And so that large gap becomes
a self-reinforcing gap,

00:08:34.020 --> 00:08:36.130
particularly in this age

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.270
when there's a change
in the economic rewards

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.733
for production workers,
the average worker.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.700
There's a hollowing out there.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:49.110
And so profit margins increase,

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:52.110
and then, as you point out,
it becomes self-perpetuating

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.740
because those who earn more money

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.840
take care of their children differently,

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:00.900
so it creates an opportunity gap.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:02.920
They spend money on different things,

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.910
and so resources of the
country go into those things.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.820
And then naturally in a desire

00:09:07.820 --> 00:09:11.490
to produce most cost effectively,

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:13.710
they go internationally

00:09:14.643 --> 00:09:17.600
and find the most cost-effective
ways of operating,

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.030
which has narrowed the wealth gap

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:23.830
between countries, but
widened it within countries.

00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:26.610
And then, also, we have a monetary policy

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.270
with the Federal Reserve

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:30.823
that buys financial assets.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:33.560
In other words, the central bank,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.920
the way it has been operating

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:38.130
when you hit zero interest rates

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:41.070
is to print money and
then buy financial assets

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:44.210
which helps those who own financial assets

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:46.500
more than those who don't.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.070
So the profit-making system
and the capitalist system

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:53.970
in which you can invest
in profitable enterprises

00:09:55.214 --> 00:09:58.600
is good, generally speaking,
as a resource allocation

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.580
but far from perfect.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:02.650
So we just have to see those outcomes,

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:04.817
and you would say, "Are we producing

00:10:04.817 --> 00:10:07.050
"a broad-based opportunity?"

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:09.860
And so it can't do everything.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:13.150
So think about, let's
say, a school system,

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:15.450
a budget for a school system,

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:17.460
it's budget based.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:21.210
In other words, they don't have capital,

00:10:21.210 --> 00:10:25.510
but yet, think about the
economic impact simply

00:10:25.510 --> 00:10:27.283
of educating children well.

00:10:28.410 --> 00:10:32.870
So the point is that it's by and large

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:34.630
a pretty good system,

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:36.900
but it's not delivering the results

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:38.400
that I think we want.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:43.400
And so my concern is that if
it's not engineered correctly

00:10:45.301 --> 00:10:46.810
to achieve that,

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:48.380
it can become less productive.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:51.050
So from an engineering point of view,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:55.080
you have to engineer it to
increase the size of the pie,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:57.820
which means increase
people's productivity,

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:01.360
because you only get to
consume what is produced.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:02.620
So to raise living standards,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:05.930
you have to increase productivity
and, at the same time,

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:08.250
so you have to increase
the size of the pie,

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:11.110
you also have to divide the pie well,

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:14.720
and it's gonna require a
better engineering system.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.290
And that is gonna have
to be done, I think,

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:18.640
in a bipartisan way.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:22.880
I'm really worried about
the battle between the sides

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:26.170
causing more problems.

00:11:26.170 --> 00:11:28.840
Because history has shown
that this is a period

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:31.030
very much like the 1930s,

00:11:31.030 --> 00:11:35.880
and when you have wealth
problems and wealth gaps

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:36.940
and difficult times,

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:40.853
there tends to be a fight
over how to divide the pie.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:44.280
- And so I'm hearing, just
to paraphrase what you said,

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:46.590
several levers that might
have maybe gotten us here,

00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:48.590
and once again, it's not
anyone's negative intent,

00:11:48.590 --> 00:11:49.910
it's just the reality,

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:53.170
technology has made knowledge
work maybe more productive

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.480
and has maybe commoditized
some labor type of work.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.630
And then you add globalization there,

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:01.070
that also puts wage pressure,
at least domestically,

00:12:01.070 --> 00:12:02.810
especially in rich countries
if some of that work

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.560
can go other places,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.020
and then that can be self-perpetuating.

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:09.300
The people who have more
are able to invest more

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:11.670
in their children and
have more opportunities.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:13.567
What do you think is the--

00:12:15.011 --> 00:12:18.010
- And the Federal Reserve,
as they buy financial assets,

00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:20.760
helps people who have financial assets,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:22.880
which they needed to do.

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:24.560
The policies that we're now seeing

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:29.150
now are more targeted
than they were before.

00:12:29.150 --> 00:12:31.550
- And that goes to a
question from Twitter,

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:33.597
Ken asks, "I wanna understand

00:12:33.597 --> 00:12:37.907
"what the Fed-printed money
or government stimulus

00:12:37.907 --> 00:12:39.830
"is going to."

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.410
And you just explained,
the Fed-printed money

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.840
is going to go buy financial assets,

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:44.950
traditionally Treasury bonds,

00:12:44.950 --> 00:12:47.827
but other things when
it gets more aggressive.

00:12:47.827 --> 00:12:49.397
"I wanna understand how the new money

00:12:49.397 --> 00:12:50.330
"is flowing to the system."

00:12:50.330 --> 00:12:51.740
So I guess you could go
over that a little bit,

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.650
but what do you think are
gonna be the main implications

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:55.920
of this kind of aggressive action

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:57.060
of where the money is flowing in,

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.680
either from fiscal or monetary policy?

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.300
- Well, thank you for that question.

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:03.310
Is it Fred?

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:04.230
Thank you, Fred.

00:13:04.230 --> 00:13:05.720
- Ken, Ken.

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:07.053
- Ken, thank you, Ken.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:13.920
Yeah, what we had before
was what I described,

00:13:14.370 --> 00:13:16.560
that the Federal Reserve would buy bonds

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.810
and then it would go
in the financial system

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:20.550
'cause the seller of bonds

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:22.770
would get the cash, and
they were an investor,

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.920
so they would buy more investments,

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:27.290
and it wouldn't trickle
down in the same way.

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:32.290
What we have now is exactly what happened

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:33.723
in the Great Depression.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:38.990
April 9th of this year was exactly

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:42.850
like March 5th, 1933.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:46.720
And in March 5th, 1933, the newly elected

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:51.660
President Roosevelt got on a fireside chat

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.760
and explained that they were going

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:57.990
to produce a lot more
money by central banks,

00:13:57.990 --> 00:14:01.140
and they were going to devalue the dollar,

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:05.850
and they were going to enter
into a lot of programs.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.193
So these programs,

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:10.500
but I should explain,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:14.913
the central government
has the right to tax

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:18.840
and spend and determine how they do that,

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.090
but it doesn't have the
right to print money.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:25.260
The Federal Reserve, the central bank,

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:27.700
has the right to print money,

00:14:27.700 --> 00:14:31.500
but they don't have the right
to determine how it's spent.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:36.000
And so in coming together,
the Federal Reserve

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:41.000
and the government, central
government, target programs.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:45.280
And so they're now targeted
at individuals which,

00:14:45.280 --> 00:14:47.230
so the government, central government,

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.330
is targeting individuals,
companies, and so on

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:51.900
in a number of ways,

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:54.930
and the Federal Reserve is buying the debt

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:55.810
so that they do that.

00:14:55.810 --> 00:14:58.700
So they're producing new money and credit,

00:14:58.700 --> 00:15:01.010
and that'll have implications
for the value of money

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:03.640
and credit, but it's much more targeted.

00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.530
So we now see those programs targeting,

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.070
of course, they're complex,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:12.060
but they really are targeted more

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:16.370
for individuals and small businesses,

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:19.510
although there are big
businesses targeted as well.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.770
So that's the nature of the dynamic.

00:15:21.770 --> 00:15:24.533
That money and credit, like
in the Great Depression,

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:28.093
will reverse deflation.

00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:30.750
I could explain if,

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.710
I worry that sometimes I
answer the questions too long,

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:36.543
but there are economic holes.

00:15:37.542 --> 00:15:41.360
You can think about it as a hole in income

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:43.780
and a hole in balance sheets.

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:46.690
And if you don't fill those holes,

00:15:46.690 --> 00:15:48.780
then there will be less spending

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.260
and there'll be asset sales.

00:15:50.260 --> 00:15:54.170
So that produces a
deflationary depression.

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:57.010
So the actions that are now being taken

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.200
are meant to fill those holes

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:01.870
for Americans, others outside the country

00:16:01.870 --> 00:16:03.920
will not have that benefit.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:07.140
But they're targeted to fill those holes,

00:16:07.140 --> 00:16:08.890
and it won't be inflationary

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:11.690
because it's negating deflationary.

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:14.350
But we're entering a
whole new period of time,

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:15.923
which I hope we can talk about.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:18.940
- Yeah, and that's talk
about that a little bit.

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:21.487
From YouTube, Rochan Sante asks,

00:16:21.487 --> 00:16:24.607
"So is it confirmed the economy will go

00:16:24.607 --> 00:16:26.767
"into recession after this coronavirus?

00:16:26.767 --> 00:16:28.747
"I'm more worried about the
recession than the virus,

00:16:28.747 --> 00:16:31.400
"because I'm afraid of jobs being lost."

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.010
What's your view there

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:35.000
of what's likely to happen as we go

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:36.870
out of the coronavirus?

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:38.160
Or maybe just what's likely to happen

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.360
over the next 18 months where
it still might be out there.

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:45.210
- I think of the virus
like being a tsunami

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:49.250
that's come, and then when it goes away,

00:16:49.250 --> 00:16:51.453
let's say it was to go away forever,

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:56.640
there's still terrible damage.

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:00.480
And the terrible damage is to incomes

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:02.023
and to balance sheets.

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:05.590
Every individual, every company,

00:17:05.590 --> 00:17:09.560
and every government has a
certain amount of revenue

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:11.980
and a certain amount of expenses,

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.360
and then they have a savings,

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:15.700
a certain amount of balance sheet.

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:19.090
Those have been severely damaged.

00:17:19.090 --> 00:17:23.360
So the loss of income,
and bring it down relative

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.990
to expenses, is necessitating cutbacks.

00:17:26.990 --> 00:17:30.250
And then the balance
sheets means great losses.

00:17:30.250 --> 00:17:32.970
Think about companies like Disney,

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:33.830
by way of example.

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.010
You think where was Disney?

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:37.930
Well, Disney will have that yet,

00:17:37.930 --> 00:17:39.460
many, many companies,

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:43.670
many individuals, many small businesses,

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:45.190
that financial damage

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.240
is a great, great damage.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:52.770
This will be the worst economic downturn

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:54.393
since the Great Depression.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:59.553
The unemployment rate will approach 20%,

00:18:00.860 --> 00:18:04.500
the downturn in the
economy will be the largest

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.700
since the Great Depression,

00:18:06.700 --> 00:18:09.920
and the financial consequences,
the financial wreckage,

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:13.880
of this will be of that magnitude.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.730
The good thing is that the government

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:21.240
is acting quickly to fill those holes in.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:24.580
However, it's not being felt

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.253
by everybody the same way.

00:18:29.002 --> 00:18:29.835
When you think of the economy,

00:18:29.835 --> 00:18:32.663
there's the rich and the poor,

00:18:33.870 --> 00:18:35.280
and it's going up and down,

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:39.070
I think we think that when it returns,

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:42.160
it's just gonna come back to what it was.

00:18:42.160 --> 00:18:44.363
There's gonna be a restructuring of that.

00:18:45.730 --> 00:18:47.340
There's gonna be a restructure.

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:51.430
People will think,
policymakers will think,

00:18:51.430 --> 00:18:53.020
who's going to pay what?

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:54.650
What bills?

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.730
And what should it be going forward?

00:18:57.730 --> 00:19:01.040
So there'll be a
reallocation of resources,

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:03.480
tax rates are going to change,

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:07.460
and also thoughts of spending to,

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:12.460
I think to establish more
of an acceptable bottom.

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.870
Because it's so disproportionately felt

00:19:16.870 --> 00:19:18.880
by those who don't have savings

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:21.700
or don't have conditions.

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.770
We can see just even

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:29.970
groups that have social contact, the poor,

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:31.870
live closer together.

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:34.770
And so in many, many different ways

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:36.513
that will be rethought about.

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.120
- And what do you think is the limit

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.490
of government action?

00:19:40.490 --> 00:19:43.440
As you mentioned, the
government can borrow money

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:44.550
in the form of Treasuries

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:48.100
to spend on programs, the
interest rate can stay down

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.010
if the Fed keeps buying those Treasuries

00:19:50.010 --> 00:19:51.370
with printed money,

00:19:51.370 --> 00:19:54.087
and just in the last few
weeks, they've issued

00:19:54.087 --> 00:19:56.690
$2 trillion of programs.

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:59.260
How many trillions can
the government spend

00:19:59.260 --> 00:20:00.590
to try to do this

00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:03.790
before you start seeing
things like inflation pick up?

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:12.970
- You have a situation where we're testing

00:20:12.970 --> 00:20:15.897
the limits of our monetary policy,

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:20.840
the capacity to print money.

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:24.010
This is very much like the war years.

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:27.390
So in the Great Depression,
there was this kind of spending

00:20:27.390 --> 00:20:29.040
and these kinds of deficits,

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:31.560
and then when we went into the war,

00:20:31.560 --> 00:20:33.170
those increased a lot.

00:20:33.170 --> 00:20:37.900
And the Federal Reserve,
in one fashion or another,

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:40.630
monetized that particular debt.

00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:45.500
The real question is whether
we can use that money

00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:48.780
productively or whether it's wasted.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:52.880
If it's productive, and
also most importantly,

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:56.113
if our society is cohesive,

00:20:57.290 --> 00:20:59.900
I think that we can get through this

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:02.690
in a managed way much like a war.

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:05.530
However, what really worries me

00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:09.003
is the fragmentation, the anger,

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:14.070
or the carelessness of
one extreme or another

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:19.070
to mess up the continued
improvement of the pie.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.430
I think there's an opportunity here

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:25.850
to restructure the system
to be in a better way,

00:21:25.850 --> 00:21:28.053
but it really requires, I think,

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.870
a cohesiveness, a bipartisanship,

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:36.370
a respect and understanding and empathy

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.690
for the various sides involved

00:21:38.690 --> 00:21:41.040
so that that's done intelligently

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.040
and the country is brought together,

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:45.220
rather than fighting.

00:21:45.220 --> 00:21:48.490
Because history has shown,
like in the Great Depression,

00:21:48.490 --> 00:21:50.920
if you go from country to country,

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.490
the reactions were very different.

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:56.770
In fact, in Europe and Japan,

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:59.900
for countries that were democracies,

00:21:59.900 --> 00:22:02.500
the internal fighting was so bad

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:06.700
that the parliaments, their congresses,

00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:11.110
chose to have autocratic
systems special rights.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:13.890
Hitler came to power 1933

00:22:13.890 --> 00:22:16.090
because the internal
fighting was so great,

00:22:16.090 --> 00:22:19.350
and there could be a
move to a more autocratic

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:22.450
and confrontational types of policies.

00:22:22.450 --> 00:22:25.270
And because this is a world problem,

00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:27.093
this is not just a US problem,

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:29.930
this is a world problem,

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:30.952
and there's competitions
going on in the world.

00:22:30.952 --> 00:22:35.060
China's a rising power.

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:38.720
And then there's a lot
of part of the world

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:41.210
that won't have the support

00:22:41.210 --> 00:22:44.420
that the United States
has because we're blessed

00:22:44.420 --> 00:22:46.740
to have the world's central bank.

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:48.410
We can produce dollars,

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.650
and dollars are a reserve currency.

00:22:50.650 --> 00:22:53.620
That means they can be
spent all around the world

00:22:53.620 --> 00:22:55.210
and they're accepted.

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:58.340
Very few currencies are like that,

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.860
no currency is like the US Dollar.

00:23:00.860 --> 00:23:03.210
So in a lot of parts of the world,

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:06.650
they're not going to be
able to fill those holes

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:08.880
that the Federal Reserve can fill.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:10.700
So we're gonna see a change, I think,

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:12.100
in the world order.

00:23:12.100 --> 00:23:14.250
And it's gonna be, there'll be anger

00:23:14.250 --> 00:23:15.280
and there could be fighting,

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:18.453
so how that's handled, I think,
is the most important thing.

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:20.374
- And that's related to
this question from YouTube.

00:23:20.374 --> 00:23:25.374
Pearper asks, "Are you
seeing any geopolitical risks

00:23:26.292 --> 00:23:28.880
"or limitations of what
the government can do?"

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.070
I mean, you're touching on this.

00:23:30.070 --> 00:23:31.430
I mean, how do you think that'll evolve,

00:23:31.430 --> 00:23:34.790
and what advice, how do we navigate?

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:36.610
You mentioned there's
polarization in this country,

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.543
there might be polarization globally,

00:23:39.779 --> 00:23:41.720
what can people do proactively

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:43.393
to get to the better outcome?

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:45.543
- Well, first,

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:49.800
I'd like to draw your
attention to a series

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.470
that I'm writing on LinkedIn,

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.760
which is called The Changing World Order,

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:56.160
and what it is is it looks at history

00:23:56.160 --> 00:23:58.260
going back the last 500 years,

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:00.930
because the same things
happen over and over again

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:03.620
for almost the identical reasons.

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:06.800
And one could see where we are now,

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:09.324
as I said, very similar to the '35,

00:24:09.324 --> 00:24:13.440
the 1930 to '45 period, or other cycles.

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:17.730
And so what happens is when
you have a great empire,

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:19.710
the world's leading empire,

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:21.940
and it becomes more vulnerable

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:24.820
if it becomes financially overextended,

00:24:24.820 --> 00:24:27.173
if the education isn't the same,

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:32.250
when rivals rise to strengthen it,

00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:35.343
that's a time that it
becomes challenged by,

00:24:36.237 --> 00:24:37.760
in other words, it can be perceived

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:40.010
as a time of weakness.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:43.890
And history has shown
that it's also a time

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:46.560
of economic difficulty

00:24:46.560 --> 00:24:50.140
and that leaders tend to be more populist,

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:54.040
more nationalist, more confrontational.

00:24:54.040 --> 00:24:57.330
And so that is why there's a tendency

00:24:57.330 --> 00:24:59.870
of conflicts, geopolitical conflicts,

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:04.650
world conflicts, that
follow depression periods.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:07.720
And so it's something that
we have to be aware of.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:10.820
There are tensions with Iran,

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.910
tensions with China, tensions there,

00:25:12.910 --> 00:25:16.920
and if it's perceived as
a time of vulnerability,

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.710
it can produce conflict.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:21.890
History has shown that to be the case

00:25:21.890 --> 00:25:22.880
over and over again.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:26.560
So in that piece, we go back to 500 years

00:25:26.560 --> 00:25:27.823
and we see the patterns.

00:25:29.210 --> 00:25:30.043
- And--

00:25:30.043 --> 00:25:33.250
- So it's something to be scared about.

00:25:33.250 --> 00:25:35.140
Now, the second part of that question

00:25:35.140 --> 00:25:37.700
is what do we do about it?

00:25:37.700 --> 00:25:41.027
The question is we.

00:25:41.027 --> 00:25:43.513
We, we're common people.

00:25:44.500 --> 00:25:47.190
We each have whatever influence we have.

00:25:47.190 --> 00:25:49.970
The most important thing is the behavior

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:52.550
of the people who have their hands

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:56.730
on the levers of power.

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:59.893
And so they will make those decisions.

00:26:01.115 --> 00:26:03.560
And so I guess we affect them,

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:07.660
and we have a right, to
some extent, to choose them.

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:10.810
And we also, hopefully,

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:13.290
don't make it so polarized

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:15.910
that we're in this battle together.

00:26:15.910 --> 00:26:19.810
And so countries dealing with each other,

00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:24.120
just like the individuals
of the political factions,

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.410
those of the left and those of the right

00:26:26.410 --> 00:26:27.623
within the country,

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:32.480
hopefully realize that the path

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:35.970
of doing it peacefully and productively

00:26:37.010 --> 00:26:41.520
is so much better than
the path of conflict.

00:26:41.520 --> 00:26:45.970
Because if, and maybe that's
the thing to pass around.

00:26:45.970 --> 00:26:48.760
Because if there's conflict,

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.130
that conflict creates its own problems,

00:26:52.130 --> 00:26:55.233
its own economic problems,
its own social problems.

00:26:56.763 --> 00:26:59.520
And that creates the
worst economic output.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.843
So it's really comes down to
how we are with each other.

00:27:04.360 --> 00:27:05.193
- Yeah.

00:27:05.193 --> 00:27:07.180
No, and speaking about that,

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:11.397
from YouTube, Andres Pineronda asks,

00:27:11.397 --> 00:27:14.017
"Please walk us through
the restarting process

00:27:14.017 --> 00:27:16.050
"of the economy," and maybe
even how that might relate

00:27:16.050 --> 00:27:18.237
to the geopolitics, but
especially the economic.

00:27:18.237 --> 00:27:20.217
"Would it be possible
to come back suddenly

00:27:20.217 --> 00:27:22.327
"from a 20% unemployment rate

00:27:22.327 --> 00:27:25.160
"while after the 2008
recovery was quite more slow?"

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.770
And what you're suggesting
is this might be a deeper

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:30.143
recession or even depression than 2008.

00:27:31.030 --> 00:27:34.120
- Yeah, I think it's
important to understand

00:27:35.680 --> 00:27:39.230
that there's a productive economy,

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.100
so if there were no financial pieces,

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:45.110
no balance sheets and income statements,

00:27:45.110 --> 00:27:48.370
there's just imagine there was no savings

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:50.500
and no financial assets,

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:52.650
that's kinda one part of the economy.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:56.440
And it works with the
other part of economy,

00:27:56.440 --> 00:28:01.090
which is the financial part
of the economy, assets.

00:28:01.090 --> 00:28:03.410
You lend somebody money,
they have to pay you back

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:04.470
and so on.

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:07.210
And they're both operating together.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:11.010
So if we didn't have the
financial part of the economy

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:11.971
and didn't have to have
a debt restructuring,

00:28:11.971 --> 00:28:16.893
you'd almost imagine, okay,

00:28:18.450 --> 00:28:21.130
you can go on and work
and the economy produce.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:24.190
Well, they can't even
produce at whatever level

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:25.380
it's capable of,

00:28:25.380 --> 00:28:28.573
and so if it was not
impaired by the virus,

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:34.540
why not go do the other things?

00:28:34.540 --> 00:28:37.890
But when we have money and we have credit,

00:28:37.890 --> 00:28:39.420
we have accounting.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.500
And so we have companies with debts.

00:28:42.500 --> 00:28:44.730
Like I live in the state of Connecticut

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:46.280
and the state of Illinois and so on,

00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:48.560
they have their own financials.

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.160
And so the financial consequences of that,

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.730
meaning that certain people

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:55.000
are gonna need payments,

00:28:55.000 --> 00:28:57.760
and when they go financially broke,

00:28:57.760 --> 00:29:01.310
then that's going to be the impediment.

00:29:01.310 --> 00:29:03.880
So the impediment to growth

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:07.630
is largely the financial
impediment to grow.

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:10.713
And that is why that's so
important to be dealt with.

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:16.683
We could all hear what
we hear about the virus,

00:29:18.709 --> 00:29:20.870
and there's a wide range of possibilities.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:25.100
It's certainly case in past pandemics

00:29:25.100 --> 00:29:29.580
that the possibility
of it coming and going

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:34.080
and not being easy to
deal with economically

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:36.327
could persist for a while.

00:29:36.327 --> 00:29:41.100
And those economic consequences
are very important.

00:29:41.100 --> 00:29:44.013
So we're gonna come out of
it with a restructuring.

00:29:45.280 --> 00:29:47.240
One day it'll be gone,

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:50.643
and we will have a
restructured economy then.

00:29:51.890 --> 00:29:53.530
Who will pay what taxes,

00:29:53.530 --> 00:29:55.533
who will have what wealth,

00:29:56.630 --> 00:30:00.070
and how the resources will be distributed,

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.260
I think, will be restructured.

00:30:02.260 --> 00:30:05.800
And how that's done will be important

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:08.470
to make sure that it's a productive way.

00:30:08.470 --> 00:30:11.160
And that restructuring is historical.

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:14.150
So, as I say, 1930 to '45,

00:30:14.150 --> 00:30:19.150
we started '29 to '38, really,

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:21.010
we had a depression,

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:24.100
and '33 they printed a lot of money,

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:25.920
and then we went into a war,

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.920
and then we had a giant restructuring.

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:31.423
And 1945 we began again.

00:30:32.635 --> 00:30:34.960
And then we were off and
had a period of prosperity

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:37.510
and that's normally the case.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:41.080
Man's capacity to adapt

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:44.870
and invent is tremendous.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:46.770
That's the greatest force.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:49.420
So while we go through
these restructurings,

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.770
I think that, at the same time,

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:54.560
there's this tremendous capacity to adapt

00:30:54.560 --> 00:30:58.210
and restructure and get
on to doing it again.

00:30:58.210 --> 00:31:00.700
So I'm confident we'll get it behind us,

00:31:00.700 --> 00:31:03.433
but I think it's probably
gonna take a few years.

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:07.510
- And on that, just to break
down some of what you said,

00:31:07.510 --> 00:31:09.370
in the financial world, when
you talk about a restructuring,

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:11.310
this is a company has some debt,

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:12.550
has to make interest payments,

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:14.750
it might have to renew that
debt after some period,

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:16.700
and then if, for example,
if you're an airline

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:18.910
and you're bringing in no revenue,

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:20.500
there's no way to pay that debt,

00:31:20.500 --> 00:31:23.200
that's the type of restructuring
you're talking about.

00:31:25.524 --> 00:31:26.357
And then, separately,

00:31:26.357 --> 00:31:28.460
there's a, I guess you
could say, the real economy,

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:31.480
irrespective of how people finance things.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.080
And that's where you're saying--

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:37.160
- Sorry, I didn't mean to interrupt.

00:31:37.160 --> 00:31:37.993
- No, no, go ahead.

00:31:37.993 --> 00:31:38.950
No, no I'm just trying to paraphrase,

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:39.783
make sure I'm understanding--

00:31:39.783 --> 00:31:42.210
- Well, on that first
restructuring, it'll also

00:31:42.210 --> 00:31:45.463
be a restructuring of who pays the bills.

00:31:46.670 --> 00:31:49.363
So yeah, there'll be the
corporate restructuring,

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:52.340
and somebody'll have an opportunity

00:31:52.340 --> 00:31:55.880
to, let's say, buy that airline cheaply,

00:31:55.880 --> 00:31:59.110
the debtors, but then
we may not fly as much.

00:31:59.110 --> 00:32:01.450
And so, I don't know,
there'll be less planes,

00:32:01.450 --> 00:32:04.540
and it'll all be adapted
to in that system.

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:07.650
And then, at the same time, excuse me,

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:09.373
and then at the same time,

00:32:10.800 --> 00:32:15.320
we're in a position where there are going

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:18.380
to have to be changes in taxes

00:32:20.160 --> 00:32:25.160
and changes in restructuring wealth

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:29.210
and those kinds of changes.

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:33.800
They may affect how states
are going to get it.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:36.520
They may how we spend money on education.

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:40.170
They may affect how we
spending money on healthcare.

00:32:40.170 --> 00:32:41.900
We're going to have to spend,

00:32:41.900 --> 00:32:43.653
we're gonna learn to save more,

00:32:45.160 --> 00:32:47.500
because a lot of entities realized

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:49.870
that they didn't have a cushion.

00:32:49.870 --> 00:32:52.840
So there'll be more
savings and less spending,

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:57.060
and it'll be reconstructed
as to how we do taxes

00:32:57.060 --> 00:32:59.083
and spending and all of that too.

00:33:00.150 --> 00:33:02.090
- And what I'm hearing from
you is you're definitely

00:33:02.090 --> 00:33:03.940
not seeing kind of an
immediate bounce-bank.

00:33:03.940 --> 00:33:06.480
If tomorrow the coronavirus
just disappeared,

00:33:06.480 --> 00:33:08.070
it's not like that everything would just

00:33:08.070 --> 00:33:09.010
get rosy overnight.

00:33:09.010 --> 00:33:11.470
There might be just the
people who've lost their jobs,

00:33:11.470 --> 00:33:12.670
they might not be hired immediately.

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:14.220
They have less purchasing power.

00:33:14.220 --> 00:33:17.100
So what I'm hearing from you
is it could be several years

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:18.693
for it to get back to normal.

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:23.240
- This will go on a while
because of those economic

00:33:23.240 --> 00:33:25.730
consequences, and then also consider

00:33:25.730 --> 00:33:27.900
that it's gonna be worse in countries

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:30.160
that don't have reserve
currency central banks,

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:31.810
most of the world.

00:33:31.810 --> 00:33:33.980
And also we're going to make an adjustment

00:33:34.900 --> 00:33:36.453
for self-sufficiency.

00:33:37.540 --> 00:33:41.140
We were on a path to
greater self-sufficiency

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:45.180
because of the rivalry,
and could-be conflict,

00:33:45.180 --> 00:33:47.010
with China and others,

00:33:47.010 --> 00:33:51.400
in terms of we're a
highly globalized economy

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:57.270
becoming much more of a
self-sufficiency economy,

00:33:57.270 --> 00:34:00.580
and even individuals are
becoming more self-sufficient.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:04.640
And that meant that the
efficiencies that we had

00:34:04.640 --> 00:34:07.560
by produce it wherever
it's most cost effective

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:11.460
to produce it and ship it
easily around the world,

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:14.220
that paradigm is shifting.

00:34:14.220 --> 00:34:18.180
So we're going from an
interconnected world

00:34:18.180 --> 00:34:23.180
to a world that is going to
be much more independent,

00:34:23.320 --> 00:34:24.309
and that will make for less
efficiencies and supply lines,

00:34:24.309 --> 00:34:29.309
for example, and so
that'll have an effect.

00:34:31.090 --> 00:34:33.693
All of those are like sand in the gears.

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:36.910
- And what's your sense,
given that this could

00:34:36.910 --> 00:34:38.950
be a protracted recession or depression,

00:34:38.950 --> 00:34:42.310
the likes of which we
haven't seen since the 1930s,

00:34:42.310 --> 00:34:44.300
you're a student of the market,

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:45.960
what's your sense of the last few weeks?

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:49.003
The market seems to be getting
more and more optimistic.

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:53.460
- Well, I think it's very
much like March 1933.

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:54.593
So let me recount,

00:34:56.060 --> 00:34:57.053
there was a bubble,

00:34:58.270 --> 00:35:01.280
the '20s were the Roaring Twenties

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:04.820
and people borrowed money
to bet on it continuing.

00:35:04.820 --> 00:35:07.250
That was very much like
the time we were in,

00:35:07.250 --> 00:35:08.520
people were borrowing money,

00:35:08.520 --> 00:35:11.890
companies were borrowing money
to buy back stocks and so on.

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:15.840
And then there was the bubble burst.

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:20.313
And so from '29 until 1932,

00:35:21.600 --> 00:35:24.190
and then the beginning of '33,

00:35:24.190 --> 00:35:28.360
1932 we had the dive,
Roosevelt was elected.

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:33.360
He was more of a populist
of the left to redistribute.

00:35:34.980 --> 00:35:38.913
And in March 5th, 1933,

00:35:40.540 --> 00:35:43.710
he announced a program that's very much

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:46.710
the same as the program that we announced,

00:35:46.710 --> 00:35:51.060
Americans announced, the
President, the Congress,

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:53.760
and the Federal Reserve on April 9th.

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:57.510
And that bottom, that
represented the exact bottom

00:35:57.510 --> 00:36:01.370
in the stock market, the
exact bottom in the economy,

00:36:01.370 --> 00:36:05.630
because it produced a
wave of money that hit.

00:36:05.630 --> 00:36:10.190
And so while we went from
the low in the economy

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:14.690
in 1932 into the beginning of '33,

00:36:14.690 --> 00:36:17.750
there was a pickup in
the economic activity

00:36:17.750 --> 00:36:20.290
that carried further along.

00:36:20.290 --> 00:36:25.290
So the Depression, reaching
its economic activity,

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:29.410
it took about 10 years for
the economy to get back,

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:30.660
a little less than 10 years,

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:32.300
and it really took going into the war

00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:34.480
to get back where it was.

00:36:34.480 --> 00:36:36.540
So I think it'll be somewhat similar

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:39.280
that what happens is there
was the big printing of money,

00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:41.820
and we call that reflation.

00:36:41.820 --> 00:36:46.190
And with that reflation,
that is what's supporting

00:36:46.190 --> 00:36:47.310
assets like this.

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:52.310
So you see both stocks and gold go up.

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:55.200
However, we're in a period of time

00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:57.903
where there's massive differences.

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.510
You talk about the
stock market as a whole,

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:04.860
but the differences between the winners

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:08.390
and the losers is enormous.

00:37:08.390 --> 00:37:10.500
And so when looking at stocks

00:37:10.500 --> 00:37:13.060
or looking at any asset,

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:18.060
we have to appreciate the
differences that exist there.

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:22.180
And also there's not much
leeway in interest rates.

00:37:22.180 --> 00:37:26.210
So the best way to get stocks to go up

00:37:26.210 --> 00:37:28.000
is to get interest rates to go up

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:29.340
so there's a floor.

00:37:29.340 --> 00:37:32.010
So I think we're in an environment

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:35.450
in which you're not
going to see the return

00:37:35.450 --> 00:37:38.860
to normalcy, but you're seeing this weight

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:42.580
of money force create the bottom,

00:37:42.580 --> 00:37:44.940
right around the same time, same way

00:37:44.940 --> 00:37:48.710
as March 5th, 1933,

00:37:48.710 --> 00:37:50.680
you're seeing that weight of money come in

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:52.250
and have that effect.

00:37:52.250 --> 00:37:54.810
And then I don't think it's gonna take it

00:37:54.810 --> 00:37:56.390
to anything where it was,

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:57.970
'cause conditions are much worse,

00:37:57.970 --> 00:37:59.940
balance sheets, income statements.

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:03.743
And then there's going to be
the value of money question.

00:38:04.890 --> 00:38:07.990
Gold and other assets
and storeholds of wealth

00:38:07.990 --> 00:38:09.660
will enter into the picture,

00:38:09.660 --> 00:38:12.200
so I think it's similar

00:38:12.200 --> 00:38:15.180
and that differentiation's
going to be very important

00:38:15.180 --> 00:38:17.580
to know where to invest
and where not to invest.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:21.990
- So it's not so much that
you're doubting the market,

00:38:21.990 --> 00:38:23.110
the market is, as you said,

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:25.030
there's this weight of money flowing in

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:26.440
and it has to go someplace,

00:38:26.440 --> 00:38:30.140
and that might be maybe
forming something of a bottom,

00:38:30.140 --> 00:38:32.560
or do you think that the market as a whole

00:38:32.560 --> 00:38:35.240
is being overly optimistic

00:38:35.240 --> 00:38:38.580
given some of the more
pessimistic scenarios

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:39.660
that might happen?

00:38:39.660 --> 00:38:42.440
- Well, if we take the market as a whole,

00:38:42.440 --> 00:38:44.057
what we have is the Federal Reserve

00:38:44.057 --> 00:38:46.580
and the federal government, I believe,

00:38:46.580 --> 00:38:50.070
saying, "We will do whatever it takes."

00:38:50.070 --> 00:38:54.070
And so if you saw that amount of money,

00:38:54.070 --> 00:38:58.200
we've seen, we're gonna
see another two trillion.

00:38:58.200 --> 00:39:01.820
I think you'll see
whatever amount of money

00:39:01.820 --> 00:39:04.783
it takes, and that means,

00:39:06.030 --> 00:39:09.520
so we're on that kind of a path.

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:12.140
The question will increasingly also be

00:39:12.140 --> 00:39:13.670
what is the value of money

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:15.850
and whether it's a storehold of wealth.

00:39:15.850 --> 00:39:19.760
And so that becomes the
next risk to worry about.

00:39:19.760 --> 00:39:24.410
So that's why I keep referring
to storeholds of wealth

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:25.960
and stocks at the same time

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:29.930
as there's that reflation going on.

00:39:29.930 --> 00:39:32.320
Because if you go down again,

00:39:32.320 --> 00:39:35.750
and the unemployment rate
goes up again, and so on,

00:39:35.750 --> 00:39:38.997
the money and credit is
just gonna keep comin'.

00:39:41.610 --> 00:39:42.470
- We could talk `for a hour.

